/* WISEBYTE LESSONS · Planet Earth: wild places and wild weather */
WB.L("uluru-kata-tjuta", "geography", "beginner", "Uluru and Kata Tjuta: the rocks at the heart of Australia",
  "A single rock rising out of a flat red plain looks like it was dropped from the sky. The real story involves vanished mountains, half a billion years and a culture that has known this place for tens of thousands of years.",
  [
    ["A rock that glows red", "Uluru is a huge, isolated sandstone monolith whose red colour is only a thin coat of rust.",
      "Stand on the plain at sunset and Uluru seems to catch fire, shifting from ochre to orange to deep red, then purple. It rises about 348 metres, higher than the Eiffel Tower, in the south of the Northern Territory, about 450 kilometres by road from Alice Springs. It is striking because the land around is almost flat, covered in red sand, spinifex and desert oaks. The base walk is roughly ten kilometres and takes most people about three hours.\n\nThe red is only a thin skin. Inside, the rock is dull grey. Its iron-bearing minerals oxidise in air and rain, so the surface has rusted. The low sun at dawn and dusk scatters away blue light, leaving warm reds. After rare heavy rain, Uluru turns silvery and waterfalls pour down its gullies."],
    ["Vanished mountains, tipped layers", "Uluru is sandstone made from long-vanished mountains, then tilted almost upright by movements of the Earth's crust.",
      "More than 500 million years ago, a mountain range in central Australia was being worn down. Rivers dumped the debris in great fans of sand and gravel, which a shallow sea buried. The weight pressed the grains into solid rock. The coarse sand became Uluru's sandstone, rich in feldspar.\n\nThe faint parallel lines up Uluru's sides are the original sand layers, which should lie flat. Hundreds of millions of years ago, movements of the crust squeezed central Australia and tilted Uluru's layers by close to 90 degrees. Erosion then stripped away the softer rock around the tougher stone. Geologists think much of Uluru continues underground, so what we see may be only a fraction of the whole."],
    ["Kata Tjuta, many heads", "Kata Tjuta is made of the same ancient mountains as Uluru, but it is taller and broken into many domes.",
      "Kata Tjuta, once called the Olgas, lies about 30 kilometres west of Uluru. The pebbles and boulders from the same ancient mountains became its conglomerate, so the two are siblings, sorted by the rivers into different sizes of stone. Many visitors are surprised that it is taller: its highest dome rises around 546 metres above the plain.\n\nKata Tjuta means 'many heads' in the local language, and there are 36 rounded domes separated by deep valleys and narrow gorges. Conglomerate, a jumble of pebbles and boulders cemented together, weathers differently from sandstone, which is why it has broken into many separate shapes. The Valley of the Winds walk winds between the domes, where the temperature drops and sounds echo strangely."],
    ["The Anangu and Tjukurpa", "For Anangu, Uluru and Kata Tjuta are living places of law, family and responsibility, shaped by ancestral beings.",
      "Long before any geologist arrived, the Anangu knew this country intimately. Anangu are the Traditional Owners, and Aboriginal people have lived in central Australia for many tens of thousands of years. For Anangu, the landscape is explained through Tjukurpa, a body of law, knowledge and story that describes how ancestral beings travelled across the land and created its features. Marks, caves and waterholes around Uluru are linked to these stories and to rules for living. Some places are sacred and are not to be photographed or visited by outsiders, which is why certain parts of the base walk carry signs asking visitors to respect them. To Anangu, Uluru is not a scenic backdrop."],
    ["Names, handback and the climb", "Ownership returned to Anangu in 1985, and the climb closed in 2019 after years of discussion.",
      "In 1873 the explorer William Gosse saw the rock and named it Ayers Rock after Sir Henry Ayers, a South Australian politician. For about a century tourism grew, and many visitors climbed a steep route up one side, despite Anangu asking people not to. On 26 October 1985 ownership of the national park was formally handed back to Anangu, who leased it to the federal government for joint management. The park is World Heritage listed for both natural and cultural values, and the Anangu name Uluru now comes first in official use. After years of discussion, the park board decided to end the climb, and it closed permanently on 26 October 2019, exactly 34 years after the handback."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "What gives Uluru its red colour?", ["Red algae growing on the surface","A thin coating of oxidised iron","Red sand permanently glued on","Rock that is red all the way through"], 1, "The inside is grey; only the surface has rusted to red."],
    [1, "Why do the faint lines on Uluru's sides run almost vertically?", ["Wind carved them into the rock face","Rain cut grooves down the rock","Crust movements tipped the layers up","Ancient people carved them"], 2, "The sand layers were once flat but were tipped nearly upright by movements of the crust."],
    [2, "How are Uluru and Kata Tjuta related?", ["Both are sandstone formed in the same river","Same ancient mountains, different sizes of stone","Kata Tjuta is a broken-off piece of Uluru","They formed in unrelated eras and places"], 1, "Rivers sorted the debris of the same mountains into sand for Uluru and pebbles and boulders for Kata Tjuta."],
    [3, "What is Tjukurpa for the Anangu?", ["Law, knowledge and story about the land","The name for the red sand of the desert plains","A ceremony held only at sunset each day","The name given to the walk around the base"], 0, "Tjukurpa links the landscape to law and story, which is why Uluru is a place of law, family and responsibility."],
    [4, "Why did the climb on Uluru close in 2019?", ["The rock became too weathered to climb safely","The park board decided to end the climb","A rockfall permanently blocked the route","The Northern Territory banned all tourism"], 1, "Anangu had long asked visitors not to climb, and the board closed the climb permanently on 26 October 2019."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-09-25","push":"Uluru is red on the outside only. Find out what's underneath."});

WB.L("lightning-thunderstorms", "geography", "beginner", "Lightning: how storms make electricity",
  "Somewhere on Earth, lightning strikes dozens of times every second. Here is how a cloud builds up enough electricity to light the sky, why thunder follows, and how to stay safe.",
  [
    ["Hotter than the Sun", "A lightning bolt heats a narrow channel of air to several times the Sun's surface temperature, and it strikes constantly.",
      "In an instant, a channel of air only a few centimetres wide is heated to roughly 30,000 degrees Celsius, about five times hotter than the visible surface of the Sun. Satellites suggest 40 to 50 flashes every second worldwide, over a billion a year. Most are over tropical land, where warm, moist air rises fastest. Lake Maracaibo in Venezuela, where lake air meets cool mountain winds, is one of the most lightning-rich spots on Earth. Central Africa's Congo basin is another, and Australia's tropical north has spectacular storms before the wet season, with Darwin famous for its evening light shows. Lightning also helps life: its heat joins nitrogen and oxygen into compounds that rain carries into soil for plants to use."],
    ["Building the charge", "Collisions between ice particles in a storm cloud separate electric charge, much like a giant battery.",
      "A thunderstorm is a giant vertical engine. Warm, humid air rushes upward, cools and condenses into droplets, and near the top they freeze. Inside, powerful updrafts throw around tiny ice crystals, supercooled water and soft ice pellets called graupel. When crystals and graupel collide, electric charge is transferred. The lighter crystals, generally positive, are swept to the top, while the heavier graupel, usually negative, gathers lower down. The cloud becomes a giant battery, with opposite charges separated by kilometres of air. Air is a good insulator, so charge keeps building until the insulation finally breaks down. Exactly how a flash begins is still an active area of research."],
    ["Leader, stroke and thunder", "A faint leader reaches down, a brilliant return stroke races up, and the heated air's shock wave is thunder.",
      "Most lightning never reaches the ground. When a bolt does, it begins with a faint, branching channel of charged air, the stepped leader, zigzagging down. Near the ground, tall objects send up streamers. When they meet, the circuit is complete and the return stroke, the blinding flash we see, surges up into the cloud. Several strokes often follow the same channel, making lightning flicker.\n\nThunder is the sonic boom. The channel heats so fast that the air expands explosively, sending out a shock wave. Distant thunder rumbles because sound from a long, twisting channel arrives at different times and echoes. Sound travels about a third of a kilometre per second, so count the seconds from flash to thunder and divide by three for the distance in kilometres."],
    ["Myths that can hurt you", "Lightning can strike the same place twice, and a car protects you because of its metal body, not its tyres.",
      "Several popular beliefs about lightning are wrong. It does strike the same place twice. Tall buildings are hit again and again, and the Empire State Building is struck many times every year. Rubber tyres do not make a car safe. A hard-topped car protects you because its metal body carries the current around the outside and into the ground, keeping the people inside out of its path. Lying flat on the ground is poor advice, because current can spread across the surface. Sheltering under a lone tree is one of the worst choices of all: the tree is a likely target, and the current can jump sideways to anyone standing beneath it."],
    ["Staying safe in a storm", "If you can hear thunder, you are close enough to be struck, so go inside and stay there.",
      "The simplest rule is 'when thunder roars, go indoors'. If you can hear thunder, the storm is close enough for lightning to reach you, because strikes can land many kilometres from the main storm. The safest places are solid buildings with wiring and plumbing, or a hard-topped vehicle with the windows up. Inside, avoid corded phones, running water and touching plugged-in appliances, since current can travel through wiring and pipes. Experts generally suggest waiting around 30 minutes after the last thunder before going back outside. This is general information, not personal advice. In severe weather, check Bureau of Meteorology warnings and follow your state emergency service's instructions."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "Where is lightning most common?", ["Over the cold polar ice caps","Over tropical land, where warm air rises","Over the cold open ocean in winter","Over dry deserts with little moisture"], 1, "Warm, moist air rises fastest over tropical land, so storms and flashes are most frequent there."],
    [1, "What separates electric charge inside a storm cloud?", ["Wind rubbing against the cloud","Sunlight heating the cloud top","Electricity rising from the ground","Ice particles colliding in the cloud"], 3, "Collisions between ice particles transfer charge, and updrafts separate the light and heavy particles."],
    [2, "You count 9 seconds between a flash and its thunder. Roughly how far away is the strike?", ["9 kilometres","3 kilometres","27 kilometres","1 kilometre"], 1, "Sound travels about a third of a kilometre per second, so divide the seconds by three."],
    [3, "Why is a hard-topped car fairly safe in a lightning storm?", ["The rubber tyres insulate it from the ground","Lightning cannot hit moving objects","Its metal body channels current around you","Cars are too low to be struck"], 2, "It is the metal shell, not the tyres, that steers current around the occupants."],
    [4, "When should you head indoors in a storm?", ["Only once rain begins to fall","As soon as you hear thunder","When lightning is directly overhead","Once the wind picks up"], 1, "If you can hear thunder, lightning can reach you, so go inside and wait about 30 minutes after the last rumble."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-09-25","push":"A lightning bolt is hotter than the Sun's surface. Here's how a cloud makes one."});

WB.L("rainforests", "geography", "beginner", "Rainforests: Earth's richest places",
  "Rainforests cover only a small slice of the planet's land, yet they are home to a huge share of its living things. Australia has some of the oldest on Earth.",
  [
    ["Into the green", "A rainforest is a wet forest with a closed canopy, built in layers like a tall apartment building.",
      "In the Daintree in far north Queensland, sunlight drips through layer after layer of leaves, and everything grows on something else. A rainforest is a forest with heavy, reliable rainfall and a closed canopy, where the treetops join to shade the ground. Tropical rainforests grow near the equator. Temperate ones grow in cool, wet places such as Tasmania and New Zealand. Together they cover a small fraction of the Earth's land, yet hold a large share of its species.\n\nThink of a tall apartment building. Emergent giants poke above the canopy, the leafy roof where most life lives. Below sit the dim understorey and a forest floor that gets only a few per cent of the sunlight."],
    ["Rich forest, poor soil", "Many tropical rainforests grow on thin soil because nearly all their nutrients are stored in living plants.",
      "You might expect lush forests to need deep, rich soil. Often the opposite is true. In many tropical rainforests, constant heat and rain break down fallen leaves within weeks, and heavy downpours wash nutrients out of the ground. The forest survives by recycling at speed. Fungi and roots spread through the leaf litter and grab nutrients almost as soon as they are released, so most of the forest's wealth sits in living plants, not the soil.\n\nThat is why clearing rainforest for farming can disappoint. Once the trees are cut and burned, the land may grow good crops for a few years before the thin soil is exhausted. A forest that took centuries to build is very hard to bring back."],
    ["Forests that make rain", "Rainforests release water into the air and store carbon, so clearing them can change rainfall and add to climate change.",
      "Rainforests do not just receive rain. They help make it. Trees draw water from the soil and release it as vapour through tiny pores in their leaves, a process called transpiration. Moist air rising from the canopy forms clouds that drop rain further inland, where the water is drawn up and released again. Scientists call these moving streams of moisture 'flying rivers'. Research suggests much of the Amazon's rainfall is recycled this way. If too much forest is cleared, parts of the system could dry out and struggle to sustain rainforest at all.\n\nRainforests also hold vast amounts of carbon in trunks, roots and soil. Clearing and burning them releases carbon dioxide and adds to climate change."],
    ["Australia's ancient rainforests", "Some Australian rainforests are remnants of the forests that covered the supercontinent Gondwana.",
      "Australia is famous for deserts, but it also holds some of the oldest rainforests on Earth. The Wet Tropics of Queensland, including the Daintree, were World Heritage listed in 1988, partly for their exceptional record of plant evolution. Many early flowering plant families still grow there.\n\nFurther south, the Gondwana Rainforests of Australia stretch across parts of New South Wales and southern Queensland. They are remnants of forests that once covered the supercontinent Gondwana, which joined Australia, Antarctica, South America, Africa and India. As Australia drifted north and dried out over millions of years, rainforest retreated to moist pockets along the east coast and the ranges, where it survives like a living museum."],
    ["Gardeners and partnerships", "Rainforest plants depend on animals such as the cassowary, and protecting the forest protects foods and medicines too.",
      "In Queensland, one of the key gardeners is a bird taller than many people. The southern cassowary swallows fallen fruit whole, including fruits too large for any other native animal, and deposits the seeds in dung, often far from the parent tree. Some plants appear to rely heavily on it. Fig trees need tiny wasps to pollinate their hidden flowers, and fruit bats carry seeds and pollen at night. Every species is tangled up with others.\n\nThe forests matter to people too. Coffee, cocoa, bananas and vanilla have origins in tropical forests, and some medicines come from rainforest plants. The macadamia comes from rainforests of south-east Queensland and northern New South Wales. Large areas are still cleared each year, mostly for farming and grazing, but protection works when enforced."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "Which part of a tropical rainforest is the dense leafy roof where much of the forest's life is concentrated?", ["The forest floor","The understorey","The canopy","The emergent layer"], 2, "The canopy is the dense roof of leaves and branches that shelters birds, insects, possums and many plants."],
    [1, "Why are many tropical rainforest soils surprisingly poor?", ["The trees release chemicals that poison the soil","Nutrients are held in plants, not the soil","It is too cold for fallen leaves to decompose","Rainforests grow mainly on bare rock and gravel"], 1, "The forest stores most of its nutrients in living plants, so the soil beneath holds little."],
    [2, "What is transpiration?", ["Plants releasing water vapour from leaves","Rain washing nutrients out of the soil","Fallen leaves breaking down into soil","Trees falling and opening gaps in the canopy"], 0, "Transpiration returns water to the air, feeding the clouds that bring rain further inland."],
    [3, "Why are the Gondwana Rainforests of Australia scientifically important?", ["They are remnants of ancient Gondwana forests","They are the largest rainforests left on Earth","They grew back after the last ice age ended","They are planted forests restored in the 1900s"], 0, "As Australia drifted north and dried, rainforest survived only in moist pockets, preserving ancient plant families."],
    [4, "Why is the southern cassowary important to Queensland's rainforest?", ["It eats harmful insects that attack trees","It spreads large fruit seeds in its dung","It pollinates the hidden flowers of fig trees","It digs up the soil to release nutrients"], 1, "Cassowaries carry seeds far from parent trees, including fruits no other native animal can swallow."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-09-25","push":"Rainforests help make their own rain, yet often grow on poor soil. How?"});

WB.L("coastlines-beaches", "geography", "beginner", "How beaches and coastlines are made",
  "A beach looks permanent, but it is really a river of sand that never stops moving. Here is how waves build, shift and carve Australia's coastlines.",
  [
    ["A beach on the move", "A beach is a temporary store of sand that waves, tides, currents and wind constantly rearrange.",
      "Picture your favourite beach on a calm summer morning, then again after a big winter storm, with the sand stripped back to a steep scarp and rocks exposed. Both are the same beach. Coastlines are among the most active landscapes on Earth, reshaped daily by waves, tides, currents and wind. Sand that seems solid underfoot is in transit. It arrives from rivers and cliffs, is pushed along the shore, and is sometimes lost offshore or blown inland.\n\nMost waves start as wind blowing over the sea, often far away. Wave size depends on wind strength, how long it blows and the open water it crosses, called the fetch. Southern Ocean storms send long, powerful swells to Australia's southern and western coasts."],
    ["What sand is made of", "Beach sand is worn-down rock, mostly quartz on many Australian beaches, sometimes mixed with shell.",
      "Scoop up a handful of sand and you hold a sample of the land behind it. On many Australian beaches the grains are mostly quartz, a hard mineral that survives long after softer minerals have been ground away. Rivers carry it down from eroding mountains, and waves sort it by size.\n\nOther beaches tell different stories. Some tropical beaches are made largely of broken shell and coral, pale and crunchy underfoot. Black sand forms where waves wear down dark volcanic rock. Whitehaven Beach in the Whitsundays is famously white and fine because the sand is extremely pure silica. Heavy minerals can collect in darker streaks, and some Australian beaches have been mined for minerals such as rutile and zircon."],
    ["Rivers of sand", "Waves arriving at an angle move sand along the coast in a process called longshore drift.",
      "Waves rarely hit a beach dead straight. They usually arrive at a slight angle and push sand up the beach on a slant, while the backwash drags it straight back down the slope. Each wave moves grains a little further along the shore, and over months and years enormous volumes of sand travel along the coast. This is longshore drift.\n\nOn much of Australia's east coast the overall drift runs northward, from New South Wales towards Queensland. Where that river of sand meets a headland or a change in the coast, it can pile up into sand spits and huge dune systems. K'gari, formerly Fraser Island, is widely described as the largest sand island in the world, built in part from sand delivered this way."],
    ["Caves, arches and stacks", "Waves carve rocky headlands into caves, then arches, then stacks.",
      "On rocky coasts, waves act like a slow chisel. They pound weaknesses such as cracks and softer layers, and hurl sand and pebbles against the rock. A crack becomes a sea cave. If caves form on both sides of a narrow headland, they may meet to make an arch. Eventually the arch roof falls, leaving a pillar of rock called a stack, which is slowly worn down to a stump.\n\nVictoria's Twelve Apostles, limestone stacks along the Great Ocean Road, are a famous example. Nearby, London Bridge was a double arch until its inner span collapsed in 1990, leaving two startled tourists stranded on the outer section until a helicopter rescued them."],
    ["Rips and a changing coast", "Rips are the everyday hazard, so swim between the flags; and building too close to a moving shore invites erosion.",
      "The biggest everyday hazard on Australian beaches is not sharks but rip currents. Water pushed up the beach often flows back through a channel between sandbars, forming a narrow, fast current away from shore. Rips can look deceptively calm: a darker, flatter gap in the breaking waves. The safest choice is to swim between the red and yellow flags at a patrolled beach. If caught, stay calm, float, raise an arm for help, and swim parallel to shore if you can. Surf Life Saving Australia has detailed guidance.\n\nProblems also arise when we build too close to the moving edge. Storms in 1974 and 2016 badly eroded Collaroy and Narrabeen in Sydney. Seawalls can starve neighbouring beaches of sand, and rising seas are expected to push shorelines inland. Healthy dunes act as a natural sand bank."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "What does the 'fetch' of a wave refer to?", ["The height of the wave when it breaks","The open water the wind blows across","The speed of the incoming tide","The angle at which a wave meets the beach"], 1, "Wave size depends on wind strength, how long it blows and the fetch, or the open water it crosses."],
    [1, "Which mineral makes up most of the sand on many Australian beaches?", ["Rock salt","Quartz","Gypsum","Graphite"], 1, "Quartz is hard enough to survive long after softer minerals have been ground away."],
    [2, "What is longshore drift?", ["Sand blown inland by strong winds","A narrow current that flows out to sea","Sand moved along the coast by angled waves","The tide covering and uncovering the beach"], 2, "Waves push grains up the beach on a slant and the backwash pulls them straight down, so sand creeps along the shore."],
    [3, "In what order do waves usually erode a rocky headland?", ["Stack, arch, cave, stump","Arch, cave, stump, stack","Stump, stack, arch, cave","Cave, arch, stack, stump"], 3, "A crack becomes a cave, then an arch, then a stack that is finally worn to a stump."],
    [4, "What is the safest way to avoid rip currents at the beach?", ["Swim where the water looks flattest","Swim beside rocks and headlands","Swim at dawn when waves are small","Swim between the red and yellow flags"], 3, "Patrolled flagged areas are chosen by lifesavers to be the safest place to swim, because rips can look calm."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-09-25","push":"A beach looks permanent. Why is it really a river of sand that never stops moving?"});

WB.L("tropical-cyclones", "geography", "intermediate", "Tropical cyclones: anatomy of a monster storm",
  "Cyclones, hurricanes and typhoons are the same storm with different names. Learn how warm oceans power them, why they spin, and what Cyclone Tracy taught Australia.",
  [
    ["Christmas in Darwin, 1974", "Cyclone Tracy destroyed most of Darwin and led to much stronger building codes in cyclone regions.",
      "On Christmas Eve 1974, Darwin went to bed expecting a stormy night. Cyclone Tracy was small, but in the early hours of Christmas Day it struck with ferocious winds. By morning most of the city's homes were destroyed or badly damaged, and around 66 to 71 people had been killed, according to commonly cited figures. Most of the population was evacuated in the following days, one of the largest peacetime airlifts in Australian history.\n\nTracy was a turning point. Investigations found that many houses had simply come apart in the wind, and building codes in cyclone-prone regions were strengthened dramatically. Northern Australian homes are now built to hold together under forces that once would have torn them apart."],
    ["One storm, three names", "Cyclones, hurricanes and typhoons are the same kind of storm; the name depends on where it forms.",
      "A storm is called a hurricane, a typhoon or a cyclone depending on where it forms, not what it is. In the North Atlantic and north-east Pacific it is a hurricane. In the north-west Pacific, near Japan and the Philippines, it is a typhoon. In the Indian Ocean and around Australia it is a tropical cyclone.\n\nAll are rotating storm systems that form over warm tropical seas, with a calm centre and spiralling bands of thunderstorms. In Australia the Bureau of Meteorology names cyclones from a rotating list. When a storm is especially deadly or destructive, its name is retired, which is why there will never be another Cyclone Tracy."],
    ["Fuel and spin", "Cyclones run on heat from seas above about 26 degrees, and Earth's rotation makes them spin clockwise in the south.",
      "A tropical cyclone is a heat engine fuelled by the ocean. It needs sea surface temperatures of roughly 26 to 27 degrees Celsius or more, through a fairly deep layer of water. Warm water evaporates, the moist air rises and cools, and the vapour condenses into cloud and rain. Condensation releases heat, which makes the air rise faster and draws in more moist air. This feedback loop organises thunderstorms into a cyclone. Over land or cooler water the storm is cut off from its fuel and weakens, though its rain can still cause severe flooding.\n\nEarth's rotation deflects air flowing towards the low-pressure centre, to the left in the Southern Hemisphere. So Australian cyclones spin clockwise. Near the equator the effect is almost zero, so cyclones rarely form there."],
    ["Inside the storm", "A cyclone has a calm eye surrounded by the eyewall, where the strongest winds and heaviest rain are found.",
      "From a satellite, a mature cyclone is a vast pinwheel of cloud, often several hundred kilometres across. At its centre is the eye, typically a few tens of kilometres wide, where air sinks, skies may be clear and winds are light. People caught as the eye passes sometimes think the storm is over, only for the winds to return suddenly from the opposite direction.\n\nAround the eye is the eyewall, a ring of towering thunderstorms where the most violent winds and heaviest rain are concentrated. Beyond that, spiral rainbands bring bursts of squalls. Australia rates cyclones from category 1 to 5 by their strongest wind gusts. Category 5 storms can produce gusts well over 280 kilometres per hour."],
    ["The real killers: water", "Storm surge and flooding often kill more people than wind, so follow official warnings.",
      "Wind grabs the headlines, but water is often the greatest danger. As a cyclone nears land, its winds pile seawater against the coast, and the low pressure lets the sea bulge upward. This storm surge can push the ocean far inland, especially at high tide over low-lying land. Some of the deadliest cyclones in history, including disasters in Bangladesh and Myanmar, killed most of their victims through surge. Heavy rain is the other hazard, and flooding can continue for days as the storm's remains drift inland.\n\nClimate change is a nuanced story. Cyclone numbers in the Australian region have declined somewhat, but warmer oceans are expected to make a larger share intense, and rising seas lift surges higher.\n\nThis is general information: in a cyclone area, follow Bureau of Meteorology warnings and emergency service instructions, including evacuation orders."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "What did investigations after Cyclone Tracy find, and what changed as a result?", ["Houses failed in the wind, so building codes were tightened","Most damage came from flooding, so homes were raised on stilts","The warnings were ignored, so evacuation became compulsory","The storm was unusually large, so Darwin was relocated"], 0, "Tracy showed how poorly houses held together, leading to far tougher building codes in cyclone regions."],
    [1, "What is a tropical cyclone called in the north-west Pacific, near Japan?", ["A hurricane","A typhoon","A tornado","A monsoon"], 1, "Hurricanes, typhoons and cyclones are the same kind of storm; the name depends on the ocean where it forms."],
    [2, "Why do cyclones weaken quickly after crossing the coast?", ["They lose the warm seas that fuel them","Mountains on the coast break up the eye","The air over land is far too humid","The Coriolis effect stops them from spinning"], 0, "Warm seas are the fuel supply, so a cyclone over land or cooler water loses its energy."],
    [3, "Where are the strongest winds in a cyclone found?", ["In the eye","In the eyewall","At the outer edge of the rainbands","Far ahead of the storm"], 1, "The eyewall is a ring of towering thunderstorms around the calm eye."],
    [4, "What is a storm surge?", ["A sudden burst of hail and lightning","A cyclone that moves unusually fast","Sea water pushed ashore by the storm","Lightning inside the cyclone's eyewall"], 2, "Winds and low pressure raise the sea against the coast, and surge is often more deadly than wind."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-09-25","push":"Cyclone, hurricane, typhoon: one storm, three names, and a reason it spins as it does."});

WB.L("glaciers", "geography", "intermediate", "Glaciers: rivers of ice",
  "Glaciers look frozen in place, but they flow, grind and carve like slow-motion rivers. They shaped many of the world's most beautiful landscapes, including some in Tasmania.",
  [
    ["Ice that moves", "A glacier is a large mass of ice that flows downhill under its own weight.",
      "Stand beside a glacier in New Zealand or Alaska and you will hear it creak, groan and sometimes crack like a gunshot, because it is moving. A glacier is a large body of ice that forms on land and flows downhill or outward under its own weight. Most move perhaps tens of centimetres to a few metres a day, though some outlet glaciers in Greenland and Antarctica race along at many kilometres a year.\n\nGlaciers and ice sheets together hold most of the planet's fresh water, around two thirds by common estimates. Glaciers exist on every continent except mainland Australia, from tropical peaks in South America and Africa to the polar regions."],
    ["From snow to flowing ice", "Buried snow is squeezed into dense ice, which creeps downhill by deforming and by sliding on its bed.",
      "Every glacier begins as snow. Where more falls in winter than melts in summer, it builds up year after year. As new layers pile on, older snow is compressed into rounded grains called firn. Over years to centuries the firn is squeezed until the air spaces close off as tiny bubbles and it becomes solid glacier ice. Thick ice absorbs red light, which is why deep crevasses glow blue, and the trapped bubbles preserve samples of ancient air.\n\nUnder enough pressure, solid ice behaves a little like stiff toffee. Its crystals slowly deform and slide past one another, and many glaciers also slide on meltwater at their base. The brittle surface cracks into crevasses, which can be tens of metres deep and hidden under snow bridges."],
    ["Nature's bulldozers", "Glaciers carve broad U-shaped valleys, bowl-shaped cirques, sharp peaks and fjords.",
      "As a glacier moves, it plucks rocks from its bed and drags them along like sandpaper. A river usually cuts a narrow V-shaped valley, but a glacier widens and deepens it into a broad U with steep walls. At the head of a glacier, ice gouges a bowl-shaped hollow called a cirque, which often holds a lake after the ice melts. Where several cirques eat into a mountain from different sides, they leave a sharp pyramid peak, as with the Matterhorn in the Alps.\n\nWhen glaciers carve valleys below sea level and the ice later retreats, the sea floods in, creating fjords like those of Norway and Fiordland in New Zealand."],
    ["What glaciers leave behind", "Moraines and erratic boulders reveal where ice once lay, and rock flour turns glacial lakes turquoise.",
      "Glaciers are messy. Everything they carry, from fine rock flour to boulders the size of houses, is dumped where the ice melts. Ridges of this debris are called moraines, and they mark the sides and furthest reach of old glaciers. Huge lone boulders sitting far from any matching rock are glacial erratics. In the 1800s, scientists such as Louis Agassiz used clues like these to argue that much of Europe had once been buried under ice, an idea that was startling at the time.\n\nMeltwater is milky with fine rock flour, which is why glacier-fed lakes often glow turquoise, as at Lake Tekapo in New Zealand and Lake Louise in Canada."],
    ["Tasmania and a warming world", "Tasmania's landscapes were carved by ice, and most of the world's glaciers are now shrinking.",
      "Mainland Australia has no glaciers today, and saw only small ones in the Snowy Mountains during the last ice age. Tasmania tells a different story. Ice caps and glaciers once covered parts of the Central Highlands and West Coast ranges. Dove Lake, beneath Cradle Mountain, sits in a basin carved by ice, and Lake St Clair, Australia's deepest freshwater lake, was also shaped by glaciers. Heard Island, an Australian territory in the Southern Ocean, still has active glaciers.\n\nWorldwide, most mountain glaciers are losing ice, linked to rising temperatures. Their meltwater feeds rivers that supply many millions of people, and shrinking glaciers may mean lower summer flows. Melting ice also adds to sea level rise. In 2019 Iceland held a memorial for Okjokull, a glacier that had lost so much ice it was no longer considered one."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "What makes a glacier move?", ["Its own weight pulling it downhill","Tides pulling on the ice","Wind pushing it along the valley","Meltwater carrying it like a river"], 0, "A glacier is a mass of ice heavy enough to flow under its own weight."],
    [1, "What is firn?", ["A deep crack in the surface of the ice","Compacted snow turning into ice","A lake that forms below a glacier","A floating iceberg that broke off"], 1, "Buried snow is squeezed into firn, then into dense ice as the air spaces close off."],
    [2, "What shape of valley does a glacier typically carve?", ["V-shaped","W-shaped","Flat and shallow","U-shaped"], 3, "Rivers cut narrow V-shaped valleys, but glaciers widen and deepen them into broad U shapes."],
    [3, "What is a glacial erratic?", ["A glacier that moves unusually fast","A turquoise lake fed by meltwater","A boulder dropped by ice far away","A type of crevasse hidden by snow"], 2, "Lone boulders far from matching rock are clues that ice once carried them there."],
    [4, "Why is the Tasmanian high country dotted with cirques, moraines and small lakes?", ["Glaciers carved it in the last ice age","Volcanic eruptions built it up","Ancient seas washed over the area","Glaciers are still carving it today"], 0, "Ice caps and glaciers once covered parts of Tasmania, leaving features such as the basin of Dove Lake."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-09-25","push":"Solid ice flows like a river and carved Tasmania. How does something frozen move?"});

WB.L("caves-karst", "geography", "intermediate", "Caves: the hidden world beneath our feet",
  "Under quiet paddocks and plains lie vast cave systems, sculpted drop by drop over hundreds of thousands of years. Here is how rain dissolves rock, and what caves preserve.",
  [
    ["Rain that eats rock", "Slightly acidic rainwater slowly dissolves limestone, opening up caves over hundreds of thousands of years.",
      "Drive across the Nullarbor Plain and you see almost nothing: a flat, treeless expanse. The name comes from the Latin for 'no tree'. Yet beneath it lies one of the largest areas of limestone on Earth, riddled with caves, some holding long underground lakes explored by divers. Such landscapes are called karst, after a limestone region in Slovenia.\n\nMost caves are made by dissolving rock. Rain absorbs a little carbon dioxide from the air, and much more as it soaks through soil full of decaying plants. This forms a weak carbonic acid, the same acid that fizzes in soft drinks. It slowly dissolves limestone, widening cracks over tens to hundreds of thousands of years into passages and chambers. When the water table drops, air fills the passages."],
    ["Stalactites and stalagmites", "Cave formations grow as dripping water leaves behind tiny amounts of calcite.",
      "Once a cave fills with air, a new kind of sculpture begins. Water dripping from the ceiling carries dissolved limestone. When it reaches the cave air, it releases some of its carbon dioxide, and a tiny amount of the mineral calcite is left behind. Drip by drip, a thin hollow straw forms, which can thicken into an icicle-like stalactite hanging from the roof. Where drops land, calcite builds upward into a stalagmite. A memory aid: stalactites hold 'tight' to the ceiling, while stalagmites 'might' reach up one day. When the two meet they form a column.\n\nGrowth is usually a fraction of a millimetre a year, so touching formations is discouraged because skin oils can stop them growing."],
    ["Australia's great caves", "Jenolan, Naracoorte and the Undara lava tubes show how varied and valuable Australian caves are.",
      "Australia's best-known show caves are the Jenolan Caves in New South Wales, where visitors have explored lit chambers since the 1800s. Scientific dating suggests the caves are very old, possibly hundreds of millions of years, making them among the oldest known open caves in the world.\n\nIn South Australia, the Naracoorte Caves act as natural traps. For hundreds of thousands of years animals fell through openings in the ground and could not climb out, leaving a remarkable fossil record that includes Australia's extinct megafauna. With Riversleigh in Queensland, Naracoorte was World Heritage listed in 1994. In north Queensland, the Undara lava tubes are a different kind of cave, formed by flowing lava rather than water."],
    ["Sinkholes and giants", "When a cave roof collapses a sinkhole opens, and some caves are among the largest spaces on Earth.",
      "As caves grow, their roofs can thin and collapse, opening a hole called a sinkhole or doline. Some form slowly as the ground sags. Others appear suddenly, swallowing roads, cars and occasionally buildings. Mount Gambier in South Australia is famous for its water-filled sinkholes, including the Umpherston Sinkhole's sunken garden. On Mexico's Yucatan Peninsula, sinkholes called cenotes open into water-filled caves. The ancient Maya relied on them for fresh water, and some were sacred places for offerings. Divers explore flooded passages linking them that stretch for hundreds of kilometres.\n\nCaves also set records. Mammoth Cave in Kentucky is the longest known system, with more than 600 kilometres of mapped passages. Vietnam's Son Doong is so vast that parts of its main passage could hold city buildings, and a jungle grows where the roof has collapsed."],
    ["Life in the dark", "Cave animals often lose eyes and colour, and cave formations preserve records of past climates.",
      "Deep caves are dark, damp and short of food, yet life has moved in. Over many generations, many cave animals have lost their eyes and pigment, since neither helps in total darkness, and developed long antennae or legs and sharp senses of touch and smell. The olm, a pale salamander from the caves of south-eastern Europe, can live for many decades and survive for years without eating. Australia has its own hidden cave fauna, including blind fish and shrimp in the underground waters of Cape Range in Western Australia.\n\nCaves also preserve climate records in their formations, helping scientists read the past. Many remain unexplored, and volunteers are still finding new passages around the world, including in Australia. They are fragile places worth protecting."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "Which acid is mainly responsible for dissolving limestone to form caves?", ["Sulfuric acid","Hydrochloric acid","Carbonic acid","Citric acid"], 2, "Rain that absorbs carbon dioxide becomes weakly acidic and slowly dissolves limestone."],
    [1, "Which cave formation grows upward from the cave floor?", ["Stalagmite","Stalactite","Straw","Cenote"], 0, "Stalagmites build up where drops land, while stalactites hang from the ceiling."],
    [2, "Why are the Naracoorte Caves so valuable to scientists?", ["They hold the world's longest underground river system","They trapped animals for ages, preserving a rich fossil record","They were formed by flowing lava, not water","They contain the world's largest known stalactite"], 1, "Animals fell in and could not escape, leaving a record that includes Australia's extinct megafauna."],
    [3, "What are cenotes?", ["Caves formed by volcanic activity","Caves formed inside glaciers","Water-filled sinkholes found in Mexico","Sea caves cut by crashing waves"], 2, "When a cave roof collapses it can expose water below, as with cenotes that the Maya used for fresh water."],
    [4, "Why have many cave animals lost their eyes and colour?", ["Eyes and pigment are useless in total darkness","Cave water slowly bleaches their bodies","They lose them to hide from predators","They are simply born without them by accident"], 0, "Over generations, features that do no good in the dark are lost while other senses sharpen."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-09-25","push":"Under the empty Nullarbor lies a hidden world that rain carved. How does water eat stone?"});

WB.L("jet-streams", "geography", "advanced", "Jet streams: the rivers of wind above us",
  "High above the clouds, narrow bands of wind race around the planet at hundreds of kilometres an hour. They steer our weather, speed up flights and once carried balloon bombs across an ocean.",
  [
    ["A shortcut in the sky", "Jet streams are narrow, fast bands of high-altitude wind that mostly blow west to east and speed up eastbound flights.",
      "Every so often a long-haul flight lands well ahead of schedule, and the pilot mentions a strong tailwind. That boost comes from a jet stream, a narrow, fast-flowing band of air high in the atmosphere. Jet streams usually sit about 9 to 16 kilometres up, near the top of the troposphere, the lowest layer of the atmosphere. Core winds commonly exceed 100 kilometres per hour, and strong winter jets can pass 300.\n\nThey mostly blow from west to east. That is why flights heading east across the Pacific or North Atlantic are often noticeably quicker than the return trip, and why airlines plan routes each day to ride the jet one way and avoid it the other."],
    ["Found by a quiet pioneer", "A Japanese meteorologist measured the jet stream in the 1920s, and Japan later used it to send balloon bombs across the Pacific.",
      "In the 1920s the Japanese meteorologist Wasaburo Oishi released weather balloons near Mount Fuji and tracked them as they rose. He found remarkably strong westerly winds high above Japan. He published largely in Esperanto, and his work attracted little attention outside Japan.\n\nIn the Second World War, American B-29 bomber crews flying high over Japan met fierce headwinds that slowed them almost to a crawl, and the jet stream became widely known. Japan had already used Oishi's findings. From late 1944 it launched thousands of hydrogen balloons carrying bombs, designed to ride the jet stream across the Pacific to North America. Most failed or landed harmlessly, but one killed six people on a picnic in Oregon in 1945."],
    ["How jets form and wave", "Temperature contrasts and Earth's spin create jet streams, which meander in Rossby waves that steer our weather.",
      "Jet streams are born from temperature differences. The tropics receive far more heat than the poles, so the atmosphere keeps trying to move heat poleward. Where warm air sits beside cold, the contrast creates a steep pressure difference at altitude, and air flows. Earth's rotation deflects that flow into a mostly west-to-east path. The sharper the contrast, the faster the wind. Each hemisphere generally has a polar front jet and a higher, subtropical jet closer to the tropics.\n\nA jet rarely runs straight. It snakes around the globe in meanders thousands of kilometres long, called Rossby waves after the meteorologist Carl-Gustaf Rossby. Where the jet bulges poleward, warm air follows. Where it dips towards the equator, cold air follows. The jet tends to steer storms along its path, so forecasters watch it closely."],
    ["Steering our weather", "A stalled jet can lock weather in place, and Australian winter rain depends on the jet and the westerlies.",
      "Usually the jet's waves travel steadily east, carrying weather systems with them. Sometimes the pattern stalls. A large high can wedge in place and force the jet to loop around it, called a blocking pattern. The same weather can then sit over a region for days or weeks: one area bakes in a heatwave while another floods. In Australia, a related feature is the cut-off low, a pocket of cold air that breaks away and lingers, sometimes bringing very heavy rain to the south-east.\n\nThe subtropical jet often crosses Australia in winter, carrying moisture from the Indian Ocean in long northwest cloud bands that can bring valuable rain inland. Further south, the polar front jet sits over the westerlies sailors called the Roaring Forties. In winter this belt shifts north, sending cold fronts and rain to Perth, Adelaide and Melbourne."],
    ["Turbulence and climate", "Jet streams cause clear-air turbulence that radar cannot see, and how climate change affects them is still debated.",
      "Jet streams are a headache as well as a help for aviation. Where fast air shears past slower air, it can create clear-air turbulence. These invisible bumps do not show on weather radar because there is no cloud. Pilots rely on forecasts and reports from other aircraft to avoid the worst patches, which is one reason it is sensible to keep your seatbelt fastened while seated.\n\nScientists are also studying how a warming climate affects the jets. Some research suggests clear-air turbulence over the North Atlantic has increased in recent decades. Other researchers have argued that rapid Arctic warming could make the northern jet wavier and more prone to blocking, but this idea is still debated and the evidence remains mixed."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "Why is an eastbound flight across the Pacific often quicker than the return trip?", ["It rides a jet stream that mostly blows from west to east","The Earth spins faster at higher altitudes","Airlines fly lower on eastbound routes","Aircraft engines work better in one direction"], 0, "Jet streams blow mostly west to east, so eastbound flights gain a tailwind and westbound flights avoid it."],
    [1, "How did Japan use the jet stream in the Second World War?", ["It launched balloon bombs towards North America","It sped up its navy with high-altitude winds","It scattered leaflets over Australia","It forecast typhoons for its fleet"], 0, "Japan used the strong westerly winds to carry hydrogen balloons across the Pacific."],
    [2, "What mainly drives the formation of jet streams?", ["Ocean tides pulled along coastlines by the Moon","Earthquakes and volcanic activity beneath the sea floor","Temperature contrasts between air masses and Earth's spin","Sunlight bouncing off clouds and polar ice sheets"], 2, "Temperature differences create pressure differences, and Earth's rotation turns the flow west to east."],
    [3, "What is a blocking pattern?", ["A storm that blocks out the Sun for days","A wall of dust moving over a continent","A stalled jet that holds weather in place","Mountains physically stopping the wind"], 2, "When a high wedges in place and the jet loops around it, heatwaves or floods can persist."],
    [4, "Why can clear-air turbulence be hard for pilots to avoid?", ["It only happens at night over the oceans","It occurs only close to busy airports","It cannot be seen on radar because there is no cloud","It is caused by wake from other aircraft"], 2, "Without cloud there is nothing for radar to detect, so pilots rely on forecasts and reports from other aircraft."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-09-25","push":"A Japanese scientist found a river of wind in the sky, and a war put it to use."});

